module gunbc.v1_maintenance_standing


// Why this carrier exists rather than a sentence in DESIGN.md: a status with a
// closed allow/refuse vocabulary is exactly the machine-consumed fact DESIGN 4c
// says may not live as prose. The freeze it qualifies is stated in DESIGN 3's
// frozen-X carve-out, which this module is cited from rather than duplicated into.

type SeedLifecycleStanding =
    FullyFrozen
  | SemanticsFrozen { maintenance: MaintenanceStanding }

type MaintenanceStanding =
    MaintenanceActive
  | MaintenanceClosed

// OPERATOR RULING 2026-08-20, superseding the behavior-shaped test below,
// relayed through deep-ant-102 (v2 self host) in direct chat with the
// operator -- there is no PR review or side-chat artifact to cite; that
// session is the record and this comment is the citation. Verbatim: "yes you
// can change it, we recently unfroze the compiler ish, basically my logic is
// anything in support of v2 self host is safe." Read together with the
// 2026-08-18 ruling above ("it is pretty frozen but fixing issues is fine"),
// the freeze is NOT repealed -- v1 is still not open for growth -- but the
// admission test changes AXIS: the governing question is PURPOSE, not shape.
// A change is admitted when it serves the v2 self-host program, full stop.
// BehaviorPreservingCorrectness / BehaviorPreservingRedundancyRemoval /
// BoundedOperationalFix / DefectRepairDiagnosticsMayMove were framed around
// behavior and defect because they predate this ruling; each one recorded
// below is a receipt that the purpose test was satisfied by a change of that
// shape, not a peer test in its own right, and the list is deliberately not
// closed to new shapes -- a change that serves the program by an unlisted
// shape is still admitted, and a change that matches a listed shape but does
// not serve the program is still refused. deep-ant-102's own reading of the
// boundary, flagged as a reading and not the operator's words, invited
// correction and none has come: capability added to the seed for its own
// sake still fails, because it supports v1 rather than v2. The refused
// classes below are untouched by this ruling and still dominate every
// admission, including this one -- a receipt: a proposed lane to author
// missing OperatorSpec/TypeCheckpoint rows so the v1 emitter could render
// constructs it has no row for was refused as SeedFeatureCompletion the same
// night, because it makes the seed more capable without advancing the v2
// frontier, and that refusal survives this ruling unchanged.
type MaintenanceAdmission =
    SupportsV2SelfHostProgram { instance: MaintenanceAdmissionInstance }

// Recorded shapes the purpose test has been satisfied by so far -- an open,
// growing receipt list, not an exhaustive membership test (see above).
type MaintenanceAdmissionInstance =
    BehaviorPreservingCorrectness
  | BehaviorPreservingRedundancyRemoval
  | BoundedOperationalFix
  | DefectRepairDiagnosticsMayMove

// The five refused classes, and they DOMINATE the admitted ones above. These are
// the growth surfaces DESIGN 3 names: a freeze that still accepts rows is not a
// freeze, and MaintenanceActive does not reopen any of them.
type MaintenanceRefusal =
    NewLanguageBehavior
  | NewCompatibilityObligation
  | NewEscapeHatchOrAdmissionRow
  | SeedFeatureCompletion
  | PublicSurfaceGrowth

data v1_seed_standing: SeedLifecycleStanding = SemanticsFrozen { maintenance: MaintenanceActive }

data v1_maintenance_standing_ruling_note: String = "OPERATOR RULING 2026-08-18, and the reason it is a reclassification rather than a loophole. The v1 seed was carried as 'frozen' under DESIGN section 3's frozen-X carve-out: no new investment, no completion work, quarry-in-place. Work then arrived that is economically correct and that the unqualified word forbids — a measured 177.8s of repeated formal canonicalization on every required-floor run, removable by construction. The honest options were to refuse the work or to state the status accurately; describing v1 as frozen while maintaining it is the rung-inflation failure of section 4b applied to a lifecycle label, and it is worse than either option because it makes the maintenance invisible. THE ECONOMIC BASIS, which is what distinguishes this from ordinary reluctance to delete: 'let it die with v1' is only an argument when some bounded event kills the cost. gunbc#8352 closed unmerged and integration/v1-cut is quarry, so no such event is scheduled and the cost is unbounded in time. Against an eight-hour implementation the payback is 28,800 / 177.8, roughly 162 required-floor runs. THE STANDING DANGER, named so it can be checked: MaintenanceActive is a candidate absorbing arm in exactly the section 5 sense — if every proposed v1 change classifies as admissible, the vocabulary has stopped discriminating and the freeze has been repealed by drift rather than by ruling. The refused list is therefore load-bearing, not decorative, and a proposal that fits no admitted class is refused rather than accommodated by widening the list."

data v1_maintenance_purpose_ruling_note: String = "OPERATOR RULING 2026-08-20, relayed through deep-ant-102 (v2 self host) in direct chat with the operator -- verbatim: 'yes you can change it, we recently unfroze the compiler ish, basically my logic is anything in support of v2 self host is safe.' THE CITATION: there is no PR review, side-chat transcript, or other artifact to point at; deep-ant-102 is the record and this data row is the citation, stated plainly rather than dressed up as pointing at a document that does not exist.\n\nWHY THIS SUPERSEDES THE ADMITTED VOCABULARY RATHER THAN EXTENDING IT: DefectRepairDiagnosticsMayMove, admitted just below and landed the day before this ruling, came from a narrower test given to the fleet before the wider ruling existed -- is the seed doing something incorrect, or failing to do something it never did. That test is now an INSTANCE of the operator's rule, not a statement of it: the governing question is PURPOSE, does this change serve the v2 self-host program, not SHAPE, does this change fit one of four behavior-and-defect-framed buckets. Adding a fifth bucket would have left the same defect one size larger. MaintenanceAdmission is amended to wrap the four existing classes as recorded instances of the purpose test rather than peer tests beside it.\n\nTHE HEDGE THAT SURVIVES: the same operator said, of the same subject, 'it is pretty frozen but fixing issues is fine' (2026-08-18). The freeze is not repealed by this ruling. v1 remains not open for growth; what widened is which shapes of change count as serving the program, not whether the seed may grow features for its own sake.\n\nA READING, ATTRIBUTED TO deep-ant-102 AND NOT TO THE OPERATOR: capability added to the seed for its own sake still fails the test, because it supports v1 rather than v2 -- deep-ant-102 stated this reading to the operator and invited correction; none has come as of this writing, so it is carried here as a stated reading rather than as the ruling's own words.\n\nA DISCRIMINATING RECEIPT, because the vocabulary must still refuse something under the wider rule or it has stopped discriminating: a proposed lane to author missing OperatorSpec and TypeCheckpoint rows so the v1 emitter could render constructs it has no row for was refused the same night as SeedFeatureCompletion. It still fails under this ruling -- it makes the seed more capable and does not advance the v2 self-host frontier -- and MaintenanceRefusal is untouched by this ruling: the five refused classes still dominate every admission exactly as before.\n\nWHY THIS CARRIER EXISTS AT ALL RATHER THAN A FOLLOW-UP TO #8594: the session that authored #8594 was archived after it merged, correctly at the time since its work was done and no ruling was pending. The ruling then moved and the authority had no owner. This amendment is not a correction of #8594's care -- it is a re-amendment because the ruling behind it changed after its author's session ended."

data v1_maintenance_defect_repair_note: String = "OPERATOR RULING 2026-08-19, narrowing the 2026-08-18 ruling above rather than reopening it: 'it is pretty frozen but fixing issues is fine.' The freeze stands; MaintenanceActive under SemanticsFrozen is unchanged. What changed is the admitted vocabulary, by exactly one class. THE GAP THIS CLOSES: the three original admitted classes are all worded behavior-preserving, so a genuine defect repair whose correct fix moves observable diagnostics fell through the vocabulary even though it is squarely inside what the operator admits — a defect is not a behavior worth preserving. THE LINE, stated because it is the entire content of the class and the thing most likely to be eroded later: the seed doing something INCORRECT is admitted, repair it, diagnostics may move; the seed FAILING TO DO something it never did is SeedFeatureCompletion, refused, and refusal dominates this class exactly as it dominates the other three. A proposal that cannot be pinned to a specific incorrect behavior the seed exhibits today, evidenced the way the redundancy-removal population above is evidenced, is not DefectRepairDiagnosticsMayMove. WHY THIS MATTERS BEYOND HOUSEKEEPING: the stale three-class reading had already produced one wrong refusal — a real lane declined on carrier strength alone — before this class existed, which is the standing danger this carrier names elsewhere (an active-maintenance arm that misdescribes itself contaminates every decision made near it, in either direction: too wide repeals the freeze by drift, too narrow refuses admitted work)."

data v1_maintenance_vocabulary_shape_note: String = "WHY THERE IS NO VERDICT TYPE HERE, since its absence is deliberate rather than an omission. An earlier draft of this carrier declared a V1ChangeVerdict coproduct pairing the admitted and refused classes. Nothing produces it: no lens reads this module, no gate joins it to a diff, and the rung note below says plainly that classification is authored by a human reviewer rather than derived. A verdict type with no producer is uninhabited generality (DESIGN section 6, model just-in-time) and, worse, it would make this carrier LOOK like a decision procedure in a PR whose entire subject is being honest about what is and is not enforced. The two vocabularies are the whole content: they give a reviewer a closed list to classify against and a name to refuse with. If a mechanical classifier is ever built for the two decidable refused classes named in the rung note, the verdict type is minted THEN, by the thing that produces it.\n\nTWO CORRECTIONS TO THE VOCABULARY ITSELF, both from review, and the first one matters more than its size suggests. A fourth admitted class, ChangeCarryingDeletionTrigger, stood beside the three above and has been REMOVED — not relocated to another axis, removed. A change that carries its own deletion trigger is not thereby semantically admissible, and treating the trigger as an admission reason is exactly the self-authorized-dissolution failure DESIGN section 5 names: a dissolution condition describes how admitted debt ends, it does not authorize creating the debt. Left standing it would have been the widest arm in the list, since almost any v1 change can be narrated as carrying a trigger, and it would have re-opened the freeze through the one door section 5 already closed. A deletion trigger remains a real fact about a change; it is a lifecycle axis carried by std.disposition, and it is not a reason.\n\nSECOND, the two vocabularies are ORDERED rather than peer, because without an order a proposal matching both an admitted and a refused class had no stated outcome — and an unstated outcome is precisely how MaintenanceActive becomes the absorbing arm the ruling note warns about. Any refusal dominates every admission. This ordering is stated and NOT enforced, for the same reason there is no verdict type: nothing here produces a classification, so the dominance is a rule a reviewer applies, at the rung the note below declares."

data v1_maintenance_rung_note: String = "RUNG HONESTY, per DESIGN section 4b, stated because a typed vocabulary is easy to mistake for a wall, and stated again here because the 2026-08-20 purpose ruling made the admission test WIDER and CLEANER and a wider, cleaner rule is precisely the shape that gets mistaken for stronger enforcement. It is not: the rung is unchanged by that ruling. This carrier sits at MITIGATABLE: it is a declared vocabulary consumed by review diligence, and nothing mechanically refuses a v1 change that belongs to a refused class. No lens reads it, no gate joins it to a diff, and the classification is authored rather than derived — so an author who misclassifies is caught by a reviewer or not at all. That is strictly weaker than the frozen-X clause it qualifies, which at least had the virtue of admitting nothing. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, which is not a general change-classifier and deliberately so: the refused classes are not all decidable, but two of them are nearly so against the seed's own surface — PublicSurfaceGrowth is a diff over the emitted seed's exported declarations, and NewEscapeHatchOrAdmissionRow is a diff over the host-builtin registry and admission rosters DESIGN section 3 already enumerates as growth surfaces. Those two climb to mechanically preventable as a diff-grain check against the prior seed emission; NewLanguageBehavior and SeedFeatureCompletion are not decidable from a diff and stay review obligations. Nothing here claims the undecidable half will climb."

data v1_maintenance_admitted_population_note: String = "WHAT THIS STANDING HAS ADMITTED SO FAR, at exact identity grain, so the population is countable rather than a category. (1) gunbc#8431, BehaviorPreservingRedundancyRemoval: one CallApplicationPlan constructed per direct call, replacing two line-for-line duplicate per-formal prologues at the same seam; measured on a same-host sequential pair with both arms built from real refs, 2900 to 2280 peel calls on gunbc.host_effect_realize and reconcile 8 minutes to 6 minutes -- and that pair is a WHOLE-CORPUS COMPILE measurement, not a required-floor receipt: gunbc#8431 carries no floor before/after receipt and this row is not the authority for one. The distinction is worth keeping because the two numbers would be quoted the same way and only one of them was measured, with 618 of the 620 removed calls (99.7%) being repeats and the distinct-work cost invariant to +0.064%. (2) RULED BUT NOT LANDED, and recorded as such rather than as an admission, because a standing that counts unbuilt work among its admissions is inflating its own record: a memo over the direct-call formal seam. Its ruled shape is an observationally transparent REALIZATION keyed on exact host allocation identity, targeting a narrow pure wrapper at the measured seam -- NOT the semantic formal-instantiation key an earlier revision of this row described, which was considered and declined because its prize is bounded by the 1.3% first-call cost while its soundness proof is made expensive by the non-idempotence recorded below. Subject, scope and retention are declared in v1.compiler.realization_contract; implementation pending, no equivalence receipt. NOT ADMITTED, and recorded here because a refused proposal is evidence the vocabulary discriminates: removing the second peel in direct_call_structured_record_literal_resolved_type_mismatch was proposed as the same class and REFUSED on evidence — a differential control over the whole corpus found peel_nominal_alias_identity is not idempotent, so peel(peel(x)) is not a redundant call and its removal would not be behavior-preserving. The control read zero counterexamples through 240,000 calls before the first appeared, which is why the count was taken to completion rather than sampled: 21 non-idempotent results over 600,000+ calls. A span-blind rerun then classified them, because v1's Node derives equality including span and a span-only difference would have had the opposite consequence: 15 differ only in span, and 6 differ STRUCTURALLY. All six are the same shape, FreeMonoid gaining a child on the second peel (one child becoming two). Since std.string_type grounds String as FreeMonoid<Char>, the doubly-peeled node is an alias expansion that appends a type argument rather than replacing it. That makes the second peel not merely redundant-but-harmless at this seam but structurally wrong for String and FreeMonoid formals, inside a function whose output is type-mismatch diagnostics. It is recorded here and NOT fixed under this standing: repairing it changes observable diagnostics and therefore belongs to a change with its own discriminating witness, not to a behavior-preserving class."

data v1_maintenance_record_literal_target_retirement_note: String = "A PROPOSED V1 CHANGE RETIRED ON SOURCE EVIDENCE RATHER THAN REFUSED ON CLASS, recorded here because this carrier's population is the record of what was proposed against the frozen seed and because a retirement for the RIGHT reason is easily mistaken later for work someone forgot to do. THE PROPOSAL (operator mandate item (b), ruled 2026-08-19): build one RecordLiteralTarget during record-literal inference, carry it into structured call compatibility, and DELETE the downstream declaration, variant, alias and field rediscovery. THE OPERATOR ATTACHED ITS OWN ESCAPE, which is the arm taken here: if source work proves the downstream path answers a genuinely different question, record that exact finding and retire the item rather than invent a unification to satisfy the checklist.\n\nTHE PREMISE HOLDS AND IS WORTH KEEPING. At a direct-call argument site the record literal IS inferred against the formal: the ExprCall arm's argument fold binds expected to Present of formal_param_type, the substituted formal, so record-literal inference genuinely sees the same formal the downstream check later receives. Anyone revisiting this should not re-derive that; it was the thing most likely to be false and it is true.\n\nWHAT IS FALSE IS THAT THE DOWNSTREAM RECONSTRUCTS THE TARGET. The two sides compute different relations over different inputs, and the difference is not a matter of degree. UPSTREAM, infer_record_lit_structural resolves a STRUCTURAL target through a four-way mutually exclusive cascade -- record_lit_instantiated_fields, then record_lit_fields_from_expected, then record_lit_variant_from_expected, then record_lit_alias_struct_expansion -- and its name test is type_name_compatible (v1.core), a PURE STRING relation: equality, a both-qualified rejection, else last-segment equality. No environment, no declaration lookup, no peel. The fact it produces is a FIELD LIST, and it exists to type the literal's field initializers. DOWNSTREAM, direct_call_structured_record_literal_resolved_type_mismatch asks application_type_names_compatible, a SEMANTIC relation over authored names: application_type_name_identity per name (lookup_type_by_name followed by peel_nominal_alias_identity), dag_can_cast, type_name_transparently_aliases_to in BOTH directions, and brand_grounds_transparently_to against the formal's declaration. The fact it produces is a NAME IDENTITY verdict, and it exists to decide whether to emit a diagnostic.\n\nSO THE CARRIED TARGET CANNOT DISCHARGE THE DOWNSTREAM OBLIGATION: a field list does not answer whether two type names denote the same type through casts, transparent aliases and brand grounding. There is a real overlap band -- names that differ textually but share a last segment, where the upstream cascade DID produce a target and the downstream still resolves both identities -- and even inside that band the carried fact is the wrong fact. Deleting the downstream derivation would not remove a recomputation, it would remove a check, which is the erased-correctness-distinction failure DESIGN section 3's replacement rule names: the minimum replacement must preserve every required refusal.\n\nCLASSIFICATION, so this does not read as an admission by omission. Carrying the target WITHOUT deleting the downstream derivation removes no redundancy and is therefore not BehaviorPreservingRedundancyRemoval; it is a shape change to the seed under a freeze, and refusal dominates. Carrying it WITH the deletion changes observable diagnostics. Neither arm is admissible, and the item is retired rather than re-scoped.\n\nTHE ONE FACT THAT WOULD ACTUALLY BE CARRYABLE, named so the next reader does not have to find it again and does not mistake it for live work: application_type_name_identity is a pure function of (type_name, type_env, module_name) recomputed per call at this seam. That is the memo class, whose implementation is REJECTED and whose draft (gunbc#8452) is closed as quarry with its design record preserved -- including that peel_nominal_alias_identity is not idempotent (21 non-idempotent results over 600,000-plus calls; 6 structural, all FreeMonoid gaining a child), which is why the neighbouring second-peel removal at this same function was already refused above. It is not reopened here."

data v1_maintenance_argv_fabrication_finding_note: String = "A SEED FABRICATION FOUND, MEASURED AS UNREACHABLE, AND NOT ADMITTED — recorded because a finding that is real in the code and dead in the tree is exactly the shape that gets rediscovered every year, and because the reason it is not admitted is more useful than the finding.\n\nTHE MECHANISM. v1_interpreter.rs push_shell_argv_tokens ends in two arms that push the format! Display rendering of the value — one for non-monoid Variants, one as the catch-all — so the Display rendering of a value becomes a host argv word. Reading Display for Value gives exactly what reaches a real process: Value::Null becomes the four-character word the four-character word null, Map becomes a brace-wrapped key-value rendering, Set becomes a brace-wrapped member rendering, Record becomes the type name followed by a brace-wrapped field rendering. That is fabricated plausible output in argv position (DESIGN §5) rather than a crash, and the four-character word null is the worst of the four because some tools accept it as a value.\n\nWHY THIS IS NOT A BLANKET FINDING, stated before the accusation rather than after: for Int, Float and Bool the SAME catch-all is correct and wanted — a List<Int> recurses through this arm per element and produces the digits the caller intends. `Delete the format! fallback` would break the good half. Only the four structured kinds fabricate.\n\nREACHABILITY, WHICH IS THE ACTUAL RESULT. No declared argv site in the tree can carry a Null, Map, Set or Record to those arms. All 258 shell-transport operations were enumerated and every argv element classified: 889 string literals, idents typed List<String> / ProcessArgvExpansion / String / NonEmptyStr / FilePath, and call expressions each returning List<String> or String. Nothing else. Both production callers — materialize_operation_argv over declaration.argv and dispatch_shell over transport.children — draw from that same declared population, and the population is entirely inside dag/. The near-miss worth keeping: extdeps.rust.cargo_build Build DOES declare `env`, typed Map of String to String inside a shell-transport operation's input block; it is an ENV input, never an argv element, and a probe matching 'operation has a Map-typed input' rather than 'this ident appears in the argv list' would have reported a live Map fabricator that cannot execute.\n\nTHE LIMIT, AND IT IS NOT DECORATIVE. All of that is at DECLARED-TYPE grain, and declared types are not construction-enforced in this tree — 3939 WhereRefinementUnenforced across 612 files, with no refinement predicate evaluated on any kernel. So this rules out an AUTHOR writing a Map into an argv position; it does not rule out a value declared String arriving as something else. The one such case anyone has actually observed — monoid-encoded string sequences, where the same declared List<String> read as one word or N depending on representation — is already closed: push_shell_argv_tokens REFUSES that ambiguity with a typed diagnostic naming both readings. For the four structured kinds there is no observed specimen and no known mechanism that would produce one, which is why the claim here is unreachable-at-declared-grain and NOT unreachable. A reader who loses that distinction will over-trust this row.\n\nCLASSIFICATION, and this is the part that decides it. The proposed change — make those four arms refuse, leave Int/Float/Bool pushing digits — is NOT ADMITTED under this standing, and it fails the test in both directions rather than squeaking past one. It is not DefectRepairDiagnosticsMayMove: that class requires a specific incorrect behavior the seed exhibits TODAY, evidenced, and an unreachable arm exhibits nothing — the whole content of the measurement above is that it cannot fire. And it fails the 2026-08-20 purpose test, since hardening a dead arm in the seed serves v1 rather than the v2 self-host program. Refusal dominates admission, so it is refused; recorded rather than fixed.\n\nTHE ARGUMENT FOR CLOSING IT ANYWAY, preserved because it is a real argument and the next reader should not have to reconstruct it: closing it is worth doing PRECISELY BECAUSE it is unreachable — the arm is a loaded default for whoever adds argv element 922, and the change converts an unreachable fabricator into an unreachable refusal at near-zero cost. That argument is not sufficient under this standing and is not meant to be; it is what makes this a row worth carrying instead of a note worth dropping.\n\nWHAT WOULD MAKE IT ADMISSIBLE: one reachable specimen. If any declared argv element is ever typed to a structured kind, or a runtime value of one arrives at that seam and is observed, the finding becomes a defect the seed exhibits today and lands squarely in DefectRepairDiagnosticsMayMove. Until then the honest name for the class is dead-code-shaped fabrication, and the honest disposition is refused-and-recorded."
